\begin{theorem}[The cap theorem, verified range]\label{thm:cap}
$\Ipair(2)=1$ exactly; $\Ipair(n)>1$ for $n\in\{4,6,8,10,12,14,16,18,20\}$; and $\Ipair(n)<1$ for $n\in\{3,5,7,9,11,13,15\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each value is the exact element of $\Q+\Q\log_2 3+\Q\log_2 5+\Q\log_2 7+\Q\log_2 11+\Q\log_2 13$ obtained from Theorem~\ref{thm:eval} by finite enumeration. The comparisons follow from rational bounds certified by integer power comparisons: $\tfrac{19}{12}<\log_2 3<\tfrac{27}{17}$ (from $2^{19}<3^{12}$ and $3^{17}<2^{27}$), $\tfrac{23}{10}<\log_2 5<\tfrac73$, $\tfrac{14}5<\log_2 7<3$, $\log_2 11<\tfrac72$, $\log_2 13<\tfrac{15}4$. Substituting in the direction dictated by the sign of each coefficient turns every comparison into a rational inequality. For instance $\Ipair(6)=\log_2 3-\tfrac19>\tfrac{19}{12}-\tfrac19=\tfrac{53}{36}>1$, while $\Ipair(3)=\log_2 3-\tfrac{10}9<\tfrac{27}{17}-\tfrac{10}9<1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Symmetry and the split-count projection}

The channel is symmetric by construction: $(x,y)\mapsto(y,x)$ preserves both the norm class and the unordered pair, so the information about \emph{which} factor carries which type is exactly zero. Projecting the type along $T\mapsto\mathbf{1}[T=1]$ recovers the classical split-count channel, whose value is $0.2947$ bits for $n=4$, $0.1487$ for $n=6$ and $0.0445$ for $n=12$ --- far below $\Ipair$ in each case, as the data-processing inequality requires. The split count is one face of the type channel.

\section{Structure laws}

\begin{theorem}[Coprime additivity, verified instances]\label{thm:crt}
For $12=4\cdot3$, $10=2\cdot5$, $15=3\cdot5$, $14=2\cdot7$, $20=4\cdot5$ and $18=2\cdot9$,
\[
\Ipair(mk)=\Ipair(m)+\Ipair(k),\qquad \gcd(m,k)=1.
\]
Numerically the identity holds for every coprime splitting with $mk\le40$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute the exact forms of Table~2. E.g.\ $\Ipair(4)+\Ipair(3)=\tfrac54+\log_2 3-\tfrac{10}9=\log_2 3+\tfrac5{36}=\Ipair(12)$, and $\Ipair(3)+\Ipair(5)=\tfrac{37}{25}\log_2 3+\log_2 5-\tfrac{898}{225}=\Ipair(15)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why additivity holds]
For $\gcd(m,k)=1$ the Chinese Remainder Theorem gives $\Z/mk\cong\Z/m\times\Z/k$ and the type factors as $T_{mk}(x)=T_m(x_m)T_k(x_k)$ with $\gcd(T_m,T_k)=1$, since the factors divide the coprime integers $m$ and $k$. The unordered pair in $\Z/mk$ is therefore determined by the two component unordered pairs together with their matching, and the matching ambiguity is exactly cancelled by conditioning on the norm class in each component, the norm class splitting as $\nu_{mk}=(\nu_m,\nu_k)$. The channel is a tensor product of independent channels, and mutual information is additive over independent products.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Doubling]\label{thm:double}
For odd $m$, $\Ipair(2m)=\Ipair(m)+1$; verified exactly for $m=3,5,7,9$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:crt} with $k=2$ and $\Ipair(2)=1$; each instance also follows directly from the closed forms.
\end{proof}

This identifies the classical quadratic bit as an exact summand of the general channel and explains the even/odd pattern in Table~2: an even order carries $1+\Ipair(\text{odd part})\ge1$ bits, while an odd order carries only the strictly sub-critical contribution of its odd factors.

\begin{theorem}[$2$-adic growth law]\label{thm:2adic}
For $1\le k\le4$,
\[
\Ipair(2^k)=\frac43\left(1-4^{-k}\right),
\]
i.e.\ $1,\tfrac54,\tfrac{21}{16},\tfrac{85}{64}$: strictly increasing and bounded by $\tfrac43$. Numerically the formula persists for $k=5,6$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute the enumerated values $\Ipair(2)=1$, $\Ipair(4)=\tfrac54$, $\Ipair(8)=\tfrac{21}{16}$, $\Ipair(16)=\tfrac{85}{64}$; monotonicity and the bound are rational comparisons.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
For $n=2^k$ the type is $2^{k-v(x)}$ with $v$ the truncated $2$-adic valuation, and $v(x+y)=\min(v(x),v(y))$ unless the valuations agree; hence $\Pi_c$ depends on $c$ only through $v(c)$, and level $k$ reproduces level $k-1$ with one extra state of half the mass. The resulting recursion $a_k=\tfrac14a_{k-1}+1$, $a_1=1$, has fixed point $\tfrac43$.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Exact channel evaluation.} Given $n$: compute $T(x)=n/\gcd(n,x)$ for all $x$ in $O(n\log n)$; for each ordered pair form the sorted key and increment the global and fibre counters, in $O(n^2)$ time and $O(n\tau(n)^2)$ space; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:count} to the global table (total $n^2$) and each fibre (total $n$); return the difference. Factoring each occupation number yields the result symbolically as $q_0+\sum_\ell q_\ell\log_2\ell$ with $q_i\in\Q$: no floating point is required.

\paragraph{Certified comparison with the cap.} From the symbolic form, bound each $\log_2\ell$ by rationals certified by integer comparisons $2^a<\ell^b$ and $\ell^d<2^c$, substitute according to the sign of $q_\ell$, and compare with $1$. This yields a proof rather than an estimate.

\paragraph{Arithmetic sampling.} For a prime $f$, enumerate primes $p<X$, compute $T(p)=\ord_f(p)$ by repeated squaring, and compare the empirical law with $\varphi(d)/(f-1)$. Chebotarev's theorem guarantees convergence; empirically the agreement is at the $10^{-3}$ level for a few $10^4$ primes.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What was actually capped}
The classical one-bit statement is correct only for two-state forks: its proof uses the fact that the unordered pair of two bits has three states partitioned into two blocks by the product character. Once the hidden variable has at least three states the counting breaks down and the true value is the finite computation of Theorem~\ref{thm:eval}. The excess is not marginal: $25\%$ over the cap for $\Q(\zeta_5)$ and $72\%$ for $\Q(\zeta_{13})$.

\subsection{Why this is not an attack on factoring}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Symmetry.} The channel discloses the unordered multiset $\{T(p),T(q)\}$; the which-factor information is exactly zero.
\item \emph{A single residue dial.} The whole channel is a function of $N\bmod f$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:thick} refining the modulus adds nothing.
\item \emph{Bounded leak.} For two-power orders the leak is capped at $4/3$ bits regardless of size; in general it grows only with the entropy of the divisor lattice, negligible against the $\Theta(\log N)$ bits needed to specify a factor. By Theorem~\ref{thm:crt} leaks in coprime directions add, they do not multiply.
\item \emph{Classical ingredients.} Cyclotomic fields, Dirichlet characters, the Chinese Remainder Theorem and Chebotarev's density theorem are all that is used; the contribution is a new measurement of a classical object.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Unification}
The quadratic split-count fork is the case $n=2$, exactly at the cap; the split-count observable for general $n$ is the $\mathbf{1}[T=1]$ projection of the type channel; the quartic pinning $2-\tfrac34\log_2 3$ is the case $n=4$ of the general formula $H_2(1/n)$; and the invariance under refinement of the modulus is Theorem~\ref{thm:thick}. Each previously isolated statement becomes an instance of a formula valid for all cyclic orders.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Coprime additivity in general]
For all coprime $m,k\ge1$, $\Ipair(mk)=\Ipair(m)+\Ipair(k)$.
\end{conjecture}
Established here for $12=4\cdot3$, $10=2\cdot5$, $15=3\cdot5$, $14=2\cdot7$, $20=4\cdot5$, $18=2\cdot9$, and verified numerically for every coprime splitting with $mk\le40$. The route is the factorisation $T=T_mT_k$ with coprime factors through the Chinese Remainder isomorphism; the missing step is a measure-preserving bijection between fibres --- a finite combinatorial statement requiring no analysis.

\begin{conjecture}[$2$-adic law and its limit]
$\Ipair(2^k)=\tfrac43(1-4^{-k})$ for all $k\ge1$; the capacity of a cyclic $2$-group channel is bounded by, and converges to, $4/3$ bits.
\end{conjecture}
Proved for $k\le4$, numerically confirmed to $k=6$; the self-similar recursion should make the induction step a finite identity between explicit dyadic entropies.

\begin{conjecture}[Even/odd dichotomy of the cap]
$\Ipair(n)>1$ if and only if $n$ is even with $n\ge4$; $\Ipair(2)=1$ exactly; and $\Ipair(n)<1$ for every odd $n\ge3$.
\end{conjecture}
Proved for $n\in\{2,\dots,16,18,20\}$ and verified for $n\le40$. It follows from coprime additivity together with the single inequality $\Ipair(m)<1$ for odd $m$, since $C_2$ is the unique quotient whose type pair \emph{is} the norm class.

Further questions: is $\Ipair$ maximised, among orders of a given size, by those with the richest divisor lattice; what is the asymptotic growth along highly composite $n$; do the same laws hold for non-cyclic abelian Galois groups, where the type becomes the cyclic subgroup generated by the Frobenius; and can the channel be defined for products of three or more primes with additivity in the number of factors?

\section{Conclusion}

The splitting type of a prime in a cyclotomic field with cyclic Galois group is a multi-state invariant with an Euler-$\varphi$ distribution, and it is a deterministic function of the Frobenius residue, so the residue-to-type channel is exact. Its binary shadow, the split/not-split root count, is strictly lossy whenever the cyclic order is composite. For semiprimes, the mutual information between the norm class and the unordered type pair is an exact finite quantity equal to $1$ bit in the quadratic case --- reproducing the classical binary-fork cap on the nose --- and strictly greater for every even cyclic order beyond it: $\tfrac54$ for $\Q(\zeta_5)$, $\log_2 3-\tfrac19$ for $\Q(\zeta_7)$, $\log_2 3+\tfrac5{36}$ for $\Q(\zeta_{13})$. The values obey coprime additivity, a doubling law, and a $2$-adic growth law with limit $4/3$. The one-bit ceiling was a ceiling on binary questions.

